    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Two mathematical models of the underground combustion process in oil reservoirs are treated in this work. The first, an unsteady state radial model demonstrates the effects of vertical heat losses on the propagation limits of the combustion front. It is assumes that the burned interval is compounded by permeable media, and that the oxidant-fuel reaction rate is infinite. For this system, a single partial differential equation is derived which account for conduction, convection and heat generation. A solution of the equation is obtained by analytical methods. The results show that vertical heat losses can lead to extinction over relatively short distances. More-over, the vertical coverage of the combustion zone is found to diminish as the radius of combustion front increases, the diminution rate being dependent upon the gas flux. It is concluded that an increase in gas injection rate can increase peak temperatures, enhance vertical coverage, and extend the propagation limit of the combustion zone for a system with a constant fuel concentration. The results of this model compare favorably, in a qualitative sense, with published data from some field tests. The second model involves a steady-state treatment of combustion in a linear porous system containing crude oil. It is postulated that the competitive processes of fuel production (coking of the crude oil) and fuel burn off are governed by chemical kinetics in a simple chain reaction. The cokes residue, in this context, is treated as an intermediate product. At first order reaction is described by a zero order reaction. A system of three differential equations descriptive of the model is then solved numerically. The results yield a favorable qualitative comparison with publishes laboratory data of other authors, particularly with regard to temperature and fuel concentration distribution. It is concluded that conditions can arise when oxygen will pass unreacted through the plane of maximum temperature. This occurs when the ratio of oxygen to fuel is large and the gas flux is high.",
